    {
      "id": 161,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        -1860,
        5960
      ],
      "size": [
        700,
        260
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 10,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "# H3 Music Video - masked song, previous scene carried as a LATENT\n\nThe cut-plan music video rendered by **Music Video Chain Render** with `carry` = *previous latent*: at every boundary the Cut Plan lets flow (onset / downbeat / lyric line), the previous scene's **sampled video latent tail** is copied straight into the new scene's latent - no decode, no re-encode, nothing lost at each link - while the song stays masked into the audio latent (the masked-audio path). This is the same carry the Short Film Chain Render uses; the earlier chain example carried decoded frames.\n\n1. Load Audio → **Sound Events** → **Cut Plan** → the writer's `cut_plan` socket. Drops, section starts, stops and your taps stay hard cuts (that is where the sync lives); onsets / downbeats / lyric lines continue the take and the writer marks those pieces `CONTINUES the previous take`. Set `continuity` to *flow everywhere* to continue at every boundary regardless (the writer then still marks only the cut plan's soft cuts).\n2. `context_frames` 39 (~1.6 s; any H3 run 5 / 22 / 39 / 56 ... works on the song path). `save_latents` writes every scene's latent to `output/apnext_latents/` so **Scene Retake** can redo one scene later, continuing from the real tail.\n3. The node's `audio` output is the song over the rendered span, sample-exact - wire it into a Scenes Join's `replace_audio` (or Save Audio) instead of joining the clips' own encoded slices, which click at every cut.\n4. Clips land under `output/video/<project>/`; the `report` lists per scene whether it continued and the trimmed head.\n\n**What H3 listens to - Voice Over Music.** The audio in H3's latent is not the song itself but a mix made for the model: AudioSeparation's **Vocals** on top at full level, **Drums + Bass + Other** summed **-9 dB** under them and ducked a further **-6 dB** only while the voice sounds. The mouth follows the voice, the picture moves on the real beat, and no word gets buried (the plain song buried consonants; the bare vocal stem had no beat to cut on). `music_db` is the dial: -3 is nearly the full song, -18 a whisper of beat. The original song still goes to the output.\n\n**Beats.** Sound Events (hits, drops, stops) → **Beat Grid** (the tempo and every beat, phase-fitted to the hits) → **Cut Plan** (the scenes, cut on the music, snapped frame-exact onto the beat) → the writer's `cut_plan` and `sound_events`. Every piece's brief then lists its `[beat]` offsets and its `[+start ->+land ->+settle s]` moments, and the scene is staged on them - the hit sentences are written ON TOP of the piece's picture budget, never instead of it."
      ],
      "title": "How this works",
      "color": "#222",
      "bgcolor": "#000"
    },
